@medcommodities Well, for a start start, those humans get paid very well! To do analysis…

One issue with AI is that all these general models (not trading specific) have really been trained on things like CNBC and slop Wall Street research. So they gave you the same type of “advice”.

This is the key insight, which was also one of the points of my tweet. 

Filtering the noise from news and analysis is the key challenge and investing today. More news and more analysis will not improve performance. In fact it will likely worsen it, because it buries the important things. It makes them harder to find and see and notice in a timely matter.

@shaxmatist Yes. Key is defining what the work is for. What the outcome is that you’re trying to achieve. 

Because certainly, AI allows you to do more. I can do things in 10 minutes today that would’ve taken me a week of reading and deep diving even three years ago.

@ekmokaya This is a key insight. “More information” doesn’t mean better. It often means an overload.

@TradeSilence That’s exactly right. But many companies are not able to measure actual performance, right? So they measure output.

@medcommodities That’s true about the “newsfeed”, but are are real trends really priced in immediately? Why is rice up +95% and silver -37% YTD then? Those moves were not immediate.

@pedrots Haha, well yes that’s the definition. More quantity of output per the same amount of labour/input.

But the output here should be outcomes/profits, not analysis. If the output you measure is analysis, then AI has definitely delivered.

@rekurencja Nice framework. Exactly this. 

The issue is that people are very bad at predefining (or even measuring) what a good outcome is.

@LordMandrake3 You don’t understand the logic? That in fund management, profits should be the outcome?

